Toward an Adaptive Morphing Protective Suit (AMPS)

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

AI Collaborators: ChatGPT, Claude, Meta AI, DeepSeek , Perplexity .

Abstract

The Adaptive Morphing Protective Suit (AMPS) synthesises six functional layers into a cyber‑physical garment capable of sensing, reasoning, and autonomously adapting to thermal, mechanical, chemical, and radiological threats. This thesis presents an integrated research programme that defines the materials, actuators, sensors, control algorithms, energy systems, digital twins, and validation protocols required to realise AMPS. Each component is formulated via controllable physical models, derives rigorous mathematical frameworks, specifies engineering targets, and maps directly onto the suit’s systems‑engineering architecture. The design is informed by a 2026 deep technology survey, which introduces clutch‑assisted shape‑memory alloy textiles, passive shear‑thickening fluid auxetic metamaterials, electroactive chemo‑responsive membranes, triboelectric energy harvesting, dual‑mode PCM‑thermoelectric thermal regulation, neuromorphic sensor fusion, high‑order control barrier functions for material‑level safety, and iterative AI‑driven design optimisation. All previously identified critical gaps—holding‑power crisis, PCM rebound heating, passive impact protection default, and formal safety at strain‑energy level—are resolved. The research is structured to progress from digital twin validation through material characterisation, morphing prototype, embedded AI, integrated demonstrator, and full‑scale field testing.

---

Chapter 1: Introduction

Protective clothing for extreme environments has historically been a trade‑off between passive shielding and wearer mobility, thermal comfort, and situational awareness. Firefighters, industrial hazmat operators, and military personnel face multi‑hazard scenarios where the threat vector can change within seconds. Conventional suits provide fixed levels of protection, often at the cost of high physiological burden and limited adaptability. The Adaptive Morphing Protective Suit (AMPS) addresses this gap by embedding actuation, sensing, and intelligence directly into the textile structure, enabling the garment to autonomously reconfigure its protective properties in real time.

This thesis defines a complete cyber‑physical system architecture for AMPS, comprising six functional layers that integrate morphing textiles, passive impact protection, chemical barriers, multimodal perception, thermal regulation, and physiological monitoring. The research is driven by twelve core investigations, each resolving a specific engineering challenge while contributing to an overarching systems‑engineering framework. The work is grounded in a 2026 deep technology survey that identified breakthroughs in smart materials, energy harvesting, neuromorphic computing, and formal safety methods, enabling the solution of previously intractable problems such as holding‑power consumption in shape‑memory alloy actuation, thermal rebound in phase‑change materials, and material‑level safety guarantees.

The remainder of this document is organised as follows. Chapter 2 presents the mathematical systems architecture that unifies all subsystems. Chapters 3–10 detail the material, actuation, sensing, control, and energy solutions. Chapters 11–13 address cybersecurity, bio‑inspired interfaces, and system integration with certification. Chapter 14 provides the validation methodology and trade study, and Chapter 15 concludes with a summary of contributions and the research roadmap.

