Chapter 2: AMPS Systems Engineering Architecture

The AMPS suit is modelled as a cyber‑physical system whose state vector

\mathbf{x}(t) = \begin{bmatrix} \mathbf{x}_{\text{phys}} \\ \mathbf{x}_{\text{env}} \\ \mathbf{x}_{\text{wearer}} \end{bmatrix}

captures suit layer states (temperature T_i, stiffness k_j, porosity \phi_k), environmental hazards (heat flux q'', impact velocity v_{\text{imp}}, chemical concentration C_{\text{chem}}, dose rate \dot{D}), and wearer physiology (core temperature T_c, heart rate HR, skin wetness w).

A protection planner solves the constrained optimisation

\begin{aligned}
\min_{\mathbf{u}} \quad & J(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) = \mathbf{w}_1^T \mathbf{H}(\mathbf{x}) + \mathbf{w}_2^T \|\mathbf{u}\|_1 + \mathbf{w}_3^T \mathbf{E}(\mathbf{u}) \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \dot{\mathbf{x}} = f(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}, \mathbf{d}) \\
& h_i(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) \ge 0 \\
& \mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{U}_{\text{feasible}}
\end{aligned}

every \Delta t = 10 ms. Here \mathbf{u} is the vector of actuator commands, \mathbf{H} the hazard cost, \mathbf{E} energy consumption, \mathbf{d} disturbances, and h_i safety constraints encoded via high‑order control barrier functions (HOCBFs) on strain‑energy functionals. The following chapters instantiate all components of this architecture while incorporating newly identified research avenues.

