Chapter 5: Autonomous Chemical Barrier

5.1 Motivation

Mode C requires a chemical seal activated only upon toxic agent contact to preserve breathability. Electroactive polyelectrolyte coatings can close fabric pores within seconds under a small applied voltage and sustain a barrier for up to 24 hours [3]. Adding cyclodextrin‑iodosobenzoate catalysts accelerates nerve‑agent hydrolysis 3–4 fold, providing simultaneous detoxification. Self‑healing polymers using dynamic covalent bonds (transesterification, disulfide bridges) [4] can seal minor mechanical breaches in the barrier automatically, extending operational life.

5.2 Mathematical Framework

The electro‑osmotic swelling pressure in the hydrogel coating is

\Pi_{\text{total}} = \Pi_{\text{mix}} + \Pi_{\text{elast}} + \Pi_{\text{ion}} + \Pi_{\text{electro}}, \quad \Pi_{\text{electro}} \propto \sigma E,

with the mixing, elastic, ionic, and electrostatic contributions defined by extended Flory–Rehner theory:

\Pi_{\text{mix}} = RT \left[ \ln(1-\phi) + \phi + \chi \phi^2 \right], \quad \Pi_{\text{elast}} = G (\phi^{1/3} - \phi/2), \quad \Pi_{\text{ion}} = RT C_{\text{ion}}.

Pore closure time \tau_{\text{close}} is estimated from Stefan–Maxwell ion migration: \tau_{\text{close}} \propto d^2 / D_{\text{eff}}. Detoxification kinetics follow Langmuir–Hinshelwood:

r = k \frac{K C_{\text{agent}}}{1 + K C_{\text{agent}}},

where k is enhanced by the cyclodextrin catalyst. Self‑healing is modelled by a damage parameter d(t) with repair kinetics \dot{d} = -k_{\text{repair}}(T) d when temperature exceeds the bond‑exchange threshold.

5.3 Technical Specification

· Base fabric: activated carbon non‑woven with poly(acrylic acid) hydrogel coating and immobilised iodosobenzoate‑cyclodextrin groups.
· Sealing time <2 s at 3 V DC, power <0.5 W; >99 % permeation reduction for NH₃ and Cl₂.
· Self‑healing: disulfide‑containing elastomer patches that close 1 mm punctures within 60 s at 40 °C.
· Weight <250 g/m²; durability: >50 chemical exposure/cleaning cycles without degradation.

5.4 Research Goals

1. Synthesise the electroactive membrane and characterise swelling kinetics under pulsed voltage.
2. Measure breakthrough times per ASTM F739 for key industrial toxics and CWAs.
3. Quantify self‑healing efficiency after repeated puncture and chemical ageing.

