Chapter 6: Multimodal Hazard Perception and On‑Body AI

6.1 Motivation

Layer 4 must fuse thermal, chemical, mechanical, and radiological data with <100 ms latency on a milliwatt budget. A phased approach is adopted: on‑body fusion of four mandatory low‑bandwidth channels (IMU, thermopile, radiation, gas class ID) with optional off‑loading of full chemical fingerprinting to a companion edge device. The AI training dataset is generated using an iterative design algorithm that refines protection strategies through three feedback loops [5], drastically reducing the number of real‑world trials needed.

6.2 Mathematical Framework

Sensor fusion is a recursive Bayesian estimator. For a low‑dimensional environmental state \mathbf{x}_{\text{env},k},

p(\mathbf{x}_{\text{env},k} | \mathbf{z}_{1:k}) \propto p(\mathbf{z}_k | \mathbf{x}_{\text{env},k}) \sum p(\mathbf{x}_{\text{env},k} | \mathbf{x}_{\text{env},k-1}) p(\mathbf{x}_{\text{env},k-1} | \mathbf{z}_{1:k-1}).

An extended Kalman filter linearises about the current estimate for real‑time implementation. The threat classifier is a quantised transformer network mapping a window of four features \mathbf{f}_t = [\Delta T_{\text{skin}}, a_{\text{peak}}, C_{\text{gas,class}}, \dot{D}] to a probability vector \mathbf{p}_{\text{threat}} \in \mathbb{R}^4 (thermal, mechanical, chemical, radiation). The loss function is weighted cross‑entropy, trained on a synthetic dataset of 10^5 hazard scenarios generated by the digital twin.

The iterative AI design algorithm [5] formalises hazardous factors and material capabilities, using a digital twin to optimise the mapping from sensor features to threat classification and response selection. The algorithm’s convergence is guaranteed via Robbins–Monro stochastic approximation:

\mathbf{w}_{k+1} = \mathbf{w}_k - \eta_k \nabla_{\mathbf{w}} \mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w}_k, \text{data}_k),

where \eta_k satisfies \sum \eta_k = \infty, \sum \eta_k^2 < \infty.

