Chapter 7: Formal Safety‑Critical Control

7.1 Motivation

Autonomous stiffening or sealing must never compromise wearer escape or cause material rupture. The safety framework extends previous joint‑angle control barrier functions (CBFs) to material‑level strain‑energy constraints, following a verified framework for hyperelastic soft actuators [6]. A haptic glove with proportional force feedback allows the wearer to override morphing stiffness within 200 ms, ensuring human authority.

7.2 High‑Order Control Barrier Functions for Strain‑Energy Safety

For a hyperelastic material with strain‑energy density W(\mathbf{F}), the safe set is defined as

\mathcal{C} = \{\mathbf{x} \mid \Psi(\mathbf{x}) = W_{\max} - W(\mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x})) \ge 0\}.

If the relative degree is m, construct a sequence of functions

\phi_0(\mathbf{x}) = \Psi(\mathbf{x}), \quad \phi_{i+1}(\mathbf{x}) = \dot{\phi}_i(\mathbf{x}) + \alpha_i \phi_i(\mathbf{x}), \quad i=0,\dots,m-1,

where \alpha_i > 0 are class \mathcal{K} functions. Then \phi_m is a HOCBF if there exists a control input \mathbf{u} such that

\dot{\phi}_m(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) + \alpha_m \phi_m(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0, \quad \forall \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{C}.

Lemma (Safety Guarantee). If \mathbf{x}(0) \in \mathcal{C} and the above condition holds for all t\ge0, then \mathcal{C} is forward invariant.

Proof sketch. By the comparison lemma, the inequality implies \phi_m(\mathbf{x}(t)) \ge 0. Given \alpha_i > 0, it follows recursively that \phi_{i}(\mathbf{x}(t)) \ge 0, and finally \Psi(\mathbf{x}(t)) \ge 0 for all t. ∎

The safety filter solves a quadratic program (QP):

\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{u}^* = \arg\min_{\mathbf{u}} \quad & \|\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{u}_{\text{des}}\|^2 \\
\text{s.t.} \quad & \dot{\phi}_m(\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}) + \alpha_m \phi_m(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0, \\
& \mathbf{u}_{\min} \le \mathbf{u} \le \mathbf{u}_{\max}.
\end{aligned}

Feasibility is guaranteed if there exists a Lipschitz continuous feedback law \mathbf{u}_{\text{safe}}(\mathbf{x}) satisfying the inequality and the control set is convex. For AMPS, the required safe input set is pre‑computed via digital twin reachability analysis, and a reinforcement‑learned safety critic provides a warm start for the QP.

7.3 Formal Verification

A subsystem‑level FMEA identified critical hazards: excessive SMA tension (fabric tear), pneumatic over‑pressurisation (bladder rupture), chemical seal failure (toxic exposure), and false sensor‑driven stiffening (entrapment). Each hazard is associated with a maximum allowable strain‑energy density W_{\max} or pressure P_{\max}, encoded as CBF thresholds. Bounded model checking using NuSMV confirms that for all execution traces up to 10^4 steps under all threat‑response combinations, the safety condition \square (W(\mathbf{x}) \le W_{\max}) is never violated.

7.4 Human‑Override Interface

The override interface translates the wearer’s manual force F_h into a desired stiffness reduction. The QP remains active, ensuring that even under full manual override, material limits are respected. A haptic glove provides vibrotactile feedback of system state, with latency <200 ms.

7.5 Technical Specification

