Chapter 8: Digital Twin and Mission Energy Management

8.1 Motivation

Phase I must demonstrate the feasibility of the 8‑h, 100 Wh budget before hardware commitment. The digital twin incorporates multiscale material models, real‑time decision‑tree validation, and energy‑flow tracking [7]. Additionally, the iterative AI design algorithm [5] uses the twin to optimise morphing strategies, reducing physical testing.

8.2 Mathematical Framework

Coupled PDEs for thermal, structural, and agent transport are solved:

\begin{aligned}
\rho c_p \frac{\partial T}{\partial t} &= \nabla\cdot(k\nabla T) + \dot{q}_{\text{met}} + \dot{q}_{\text{rad}} + \dot{q}_{\text{Joule}}, \\
\nabla\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma} + \mathbf{b} &= \rho\ddot{\mathbf{u}}, \\
\frac{\partial C}{\partial t} + \mathbf{v}\cdot\nabla C &= D\nabla^2 C - R(C).
\end{aligned}

The mission‑power model computes total energy for a sequence of N events:

E_{\text{total}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left( E_{\text{deploy},i} + P_{\text{hold},i} \Delta t_{\text{hold},i} \right) + P_{\text{sens+AI}} T_{\text{mission}},

with E_{\text{deploy},i} \approx 5 J per SMA activation, P_{\text{hold},i} < 0.5 W, and P_{\text{sens+AI}} \approx 50 mW. The constraint E_{\text{total}} \le 100 Wh is enforced.

Reduced‑order models are built via proper orthogonal decomposition (POD). The error in the energy norm is bounded by the sum of neglected singular values:

\| \mathbf{u}(t) - \mathbf{u}_r(t) \|_E \le \sum_{i=r+1}^{M} \sigma_i,

guaranteeing that energy balance is preserved within 5 % if r is chosen such that the cumulative energy of truncated modes is below 2.5 %.

8.3 Digital Twin Workflow

The HIL (hardware‑in‑the‑loop) architecture comprises:

```
[Operational Scenario] → [Threat Sequence Generator]
          ↓
[Multiphysics FEM (CalculiX + OpenFOAM)] → [Reduced Order Model (POD)]
          ↓
[Mission Power Integrator (Simulink)] → [Battery Model]
          ↓
[AI Decision Logic (TensorFlow Lite)] ← [AMPS AI Board (HIL)]
          ↓
[Visualisation & Endurance Prediction]
```

The twin runs faster than real time (10×) on a workstation, providing state updates every virtual 10 ms, and validates control algorithms, power usage, and safety filter under thousands of hazard sequences before any physical actuator is installed.

