Chapter 9: Adaptive Thermal Regulation

9.1 Motivation

Layer 5 must maintain thermal comfort across −40 °C to +250 °C transients. The previous PCM‑only solution risks rebound heating during solidification. The revised system pairs microencapsulated paraffin PCM with thin‑film thermoelectric coolers (TECs) that pump released latent heat away from the skin. For rapid, autonomous ventilation modulation, graphene‑enabled electroresponsive textiles adjust porosity in response to biometric feedback [8].

9.2 Mathematical Framework

Lumped heat balance including TEC:

\rho c_p \frac{dT}{dt} = \dot{q}_{\text{met}} + \dot{q}_{\text{ext}} - \dot{q}_{\text{PCM}} - \dot{q}_{\text{TEC}},

with \dot{q}_{\text{TEC}} = \eta_{\text{TE}} P_{\text{TEC}} and the thermoelectric efficiency

\eta_{\text{TE}} = \frac{T_c}{T_h - T_c} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{1 + Z \bar{T}} - 1}{\sqrt{1 + Z \bar{T}} + T_h/T_c},

where Z is the figure of merit, T_h, T_c hot/cold side temperatures, and \bar{T} their average.

PCM enthalpy is modelled via apparent heat capacity:

c_p^{\text{eff}}(T) = c_{p,s} + \frac{L}{\Delta T} \cdot \frac{1}{2} \left[ 1 + \tanh\left( \frac{2(T - T_m)}{\Delta T} \right) \right],

with L latent heat, T_m melting point, and \Delta T the phase‑change temperature window. The TEC is activated only during the cooling phase (solidification) to remove released heat and limit skin temperature rise to <1 °C.

The graphene electroresponsive fabric modulates porosity \phi through reversible hydrogen bonding; local convective heat dissipation scales with \phi and skin temperature, and emissivity can be tuned from 0.2 to 0.9 with <1 mW.

9.3 Technical Specification

