Chapter 10: Soft Actuators and Textile Metasurfaces

10.1 Motivation

For low‑force morphing tasks (e.g., neck seal closure, ventilation flap actuation), fabric McKibben muscles and electroactive yarns are employed. Structurally programmed textile metasurfaces [9] allow non‑Euclidean shape changes (e.g., Gaussian curvature morphing) through stitch‑level geometric design, eliminating the need for bulky pneumatic bladders.

10.2 Mathematical Framework

The static force of a fabric McKibben actuator is

F = \frac{\pi D_0^2 P}{4} \left( \frac{3\cos^2\theta_0 - 1}{\sin^2\theta_0} \right).

Electroactive yarn linear strain scales as \varepsilon \propto Q^2. The metasurface design encodes target 3D shapes into 2D knitting patterns; the transformation from flat to target shape is governed by the programmed metric tensor \bar{g}_{\alpha\beta}.

