A.3 Coupled Multiphysics PDEs for the Digital Twin

The suit’s digital twin solves three coupled boundary value problems:

1. Heat transfer (across Layers 1–5)

\rho c_p \frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \nabla \cdot (k \nabla T) + \dot{q}_{\text{met}} + \dot{q}_{\text{rad}} + \dot{q}_{\text{Joule}},

with \dot{q}_{\text{met}} metabolic heat from the wearer, \dot{q}_{\text{rad}} external radiative flux, and \dot{q}_{\text{Joule}} actuator heating.

2. Structural mechanics (morphing and impact)

\nabla \cdot \boldsymbol{\sigma} + \mathbf{b} = \rho \ddot{\mathbf{u}},

where \mathbf{b} body forces, \boldsymbol{\sigma} stress from hyperelastic‑plastic material models (for SMA and STF‑auxetic layers).

3. Agent transport (chemical and radiological)

\frac{\partial C}{\partial t} + \mathbf{v} \cdot \nabla C = D \nabla^2 C - R(C) + S,

with C agent concentration, D diffusivity through fabric, R(C) reaction term (detoxification), and S source term at the outer surface.

These are discretised using the finite element method (CalculiX for mechanics, custom OpenFOAM solver for transport) and coupled via a weak formulation across interfaces.

---

A.4 PCM–Thermoelectric Thermal Management Model

The lumped‑parameter energy balance for the torso section is

(\rho c_p)_{\text{eff}} \frac{dT}{dt} = \dot{Q}_{\text{met}} + \dot{Q}_{\text{ext}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{PCM}} - \dot{Q}_{\text{TEC}},

with

\dot{Q}_{\text{PCM}} = \frac{d}{dt} \int_{V} \rho h(T) \, dV, \quad h(T) = \int_{T_0}^T c_p^{\text{eff}}(\tau) d\tau,

\dot{Q}_{\text{TEC}} = \eta_{\text{TE}} P_{\text{TEC}}, \quad \eta_{\text{TE}} = \frac{T_c}{T_h - T_c} \cdot \frac{\sqrt{1 + Z \bar{T}} - 1}{\sqrt{1 + Z \bar{T}} + T_h/T_c},

where Z is the thermoelectric figure of merit, T_h and T_c hot/cold side temperatures, and \bar{T} their average. The PCM effective specific heat is modelled as a smoothed step function:

c_p^{\text{eff}}(T) = c_{p,s} + \frac{L}{\Delta T} \cdot \frac{1}{2} \left[ 1 + \tanh\left( \frac{2(T - T_m)}{\Delta T} \right) \right],

with L latent heat, T_m melting point, and \Delta T phase‑change temperature window. The TEC is activated only when dT/dt > 0 during the cooling phase (solidification) to remove released heat and keep the core temperature stable.

---

A.5 Shear‑Thickening Fluid Impregnated Auxetic Metamaterial

The effective Poisson’s ratio for a 2D double‑arrowhead unit cell is derived from geometry:

\nu_{xy} = -\frac{\epsilon_y}{\epsilon_x} = -\frac{\sin\theta\,(h/l + \sin\theta)}{\cos^2\theta},

where \theta, h, and l are the cell geometric parameters. The rate‑dependent stress of the STF‑impregnated yarn is

\sigma(\varepsilon, \dot{\varepsilon}) = \sigma_0(\varepsilon) + \eta(\dot{\varepsilon})\,\dot{\varepsilon},

\eta(\dot{\varepsilon}) = \eta_0 + \eta_1 \dot{\varepsilon}^n \quad (n \approx 0.3\text{–}0.5),

with the STF exhibiting shear‑thickening for \dot{\varepsilon} > \dot{\varepsilon}_c. Specific energy absorption (SEA) up to densification strain \varepsilon_{\text{lock}} is

\text{SEA} = \frac{1}{\rho_{\text{cell}}} \int_{0}^{\varepsilon_{\text{lock}}} \sigma(\varepsilon, \dot{\varepsilon})\,d\varepsilon.

The design variables \{\theta, h, l, t_{\text{strut}}\} are optimised via a genetic algorithm to maximise SEA while keeping the low‑rate elastic modulus below 1 MPa for comfort.

---

A.6 Electroactive Chemo‑Responsive Membrane

The hydrogel swelling pressure is given by the Flory–Rehner theory extended with an electroosmotic term:

\Pi_{\text{total}} = \underbrace{RT \left[ \ln(1-\phi) + \phi + \chi \phi^2 \right]}_{\Pi_{\text{mix}}} + \underbrace{G (\phi^{1/3} - \phi/2)}_{\Pi_{\text{elast}}} + \underbrace{RT C_{\text{ion}}}_{\Pi_{\text{ion}}} + \underbrace{f \sigma E}_{\Pi_{\text{electro}}},

where \phi polymer volume fraction, \chi Flory interaction parameter, G shear modulus, C_{\text{ion}} mobile ion concentration, f efficiency factor, \sigma fixed charge density, and E applied electric field. Pore closure time \tau_{\text{close}} is estimated from the Stefan–Maxwell equations for ion migration across the coating thickness, yielding \tau_{\text{close}} \propto d^2 / (D_{\text{eff}}), with D_{\text{eff}} the effective ion diffusivity. At 3 V DC, \tau_{\text{close}} < 2 s has been demonstrated.

---

A.7 Sensor Fusion and Threat Classification

The recursive Bayesian filter updates the environmental state belief:

\text{Bel}(\mathbf{x}_k) = \eta\, p(\mathbf{z}_k|\mathbf{x}_k) \int p(\mathbf{x}_k|\mathbf{x}_{k-1}) \, \text{Bel}(\mathbf{x}_{k-1})\, d\mathbf{x}_{k-1}.

For low‑dimensional states, an extended Kalman filter (EKF) linearises about the current estimate. The threat classifier is a quantised transformer network that maps a window of four features \mathbf{f}_t = [\Delta T_{\text{skin}}, a_{\text{peak}}, C_{\text{gas,class}}, \dot{D}] to a probability vector \mathbf{p}_{\text{threat}} \in \mathbb{R}^4 (thermal, mechanical, chemical, radiation). The loss function is weighted cross‑entropy, trained on a synthetic dataset generated by the digital twin under 10^5 hazard scenarios.

---

A.8 Mission Power Model

For a mission sequence of N events, the total energy consumed is

E_{\text{total}} = \sum_{i=1}^{N} \left( E_{\text{deploy},i} + P_{\text{hold},i} \Delta t_{\text{hold},i} \right) + P_{\text{sens+AI}} T_{\text{mission}},

where E_{\text{deploy},i} is the energy for one SMA activation (\approx 5 J), P_{\text{hold},i} the clutch or bistable hold power (<0.5 W), and P_{\text{sens+AI}} \approx 50 mW continuous. The constraint E_{\text{total}} \le 100 Wh is enforced. The digital twin simulates worst‑case scenarios to verify compliance.

---

A.9 Reduced‑Order Model Error Bounds via POD

A proper orthogonal decomposition (POD) reduces the high‑dimensional FEM state \mathbf{u}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^M to a low‑dimensional subspace \mathbf{u}(t) \approx \sum_{i=1}^{r} a_i(t) \boldsymbol{\phi}_i. The error in the energy norm is bounded by the sum of neglected singular values:

\| \mathbf{u}(t) - \mathbf{u}_r(t) \|_E \le \sum_{i=r+1}^{M} \sigma_i.

For the power prediction, the reduced model’s error in total energy is propagated via linear sensitivity analysis, guaranteeing that the 5 % requirement is met if r is chosen such that the cumulative energy of the truncated modes is below 2.5 %. This is verified a priori on representative load cases.

