Appendix D — Formal Verification of the Safety Filter

D.1 Hazard and Failure Mode Analysis

A subsystem‑level FMEA (Failure Modes and Effects Analysis) was conducted for the morphing and chemical sealing layers. Critical hazards include:

· H1: Excessive SMA tension → fabric tear → loss of thermal stand‑off.
· H2: Over‑pressurisation of pneumatic artificial muscle → bladder rupture → impact on wearer.
· H3: Chemical seal fails to close due to electrode delamination → toxic exposure.
· H4: False sensor reading causes unnecessary stiffening → wearer entrapment.

For each hazard, the maximum allowable strain‑energy density W_{\max} or over‑pressure P_{\max} is defined. These become the CBF thresholds.

D.2 Model Checking Approach

The HOCBF‑based safety filter is translated into a linear temporal logic (LTL) specification: \square (W(\mathbf{x}) \le W_{\max}). A discrete‑time abstraction of the closed‑loop system (including the QP) is subjected to bounded model checking (BMC) using the NuSMV tool. The digital twin provides the dynamical model’s state transition relation for all possible threat‑response combinations. The BMC confirms that for all execution traces up to a depth of 10^4 steps, the safety condition is never violated.

D.3 Proof of QP Feasibility

Theorem. Given the system \dot{\mathbf{x}} = f(\mathbf{x}) + g(\mathbf{x})\mathbf{u} with relative degree m for h(\mathbf{x}), if \mathcal{C} is non‑empty and there exists a Lipschitz continuous feedback law \mathbf{u}_{\text{safe}}(\mathbf{x}) that satisfies the HOCBF inequality for all \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{C}, then the quadratic program

\mathbf{u}^* = \arg\min_{\mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{U}} \|\mathbf{u} - \mathbf{u}_{\text{des}}\|^2 \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \dot{\psi}_m + \alpha_m \psi_m \ge 0

is feasible for every \mathbf{u}_{\text{des}} \in \mathbb{R}^p, provided \mathcal{U} is convex and the safe input set has non‑empty intersection with \mathcal{U}.

Proof. Because \mathbf{u}_{\text{safe}} is a valid control that renders the constraint active, the constraint set \Omega(\mathbf{x}) = \{\mathbf{u} \in \mathcal{U} \mid \dot{\psi}_m(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{u}) + \alpha_m \psi_m(\mathbf{x}) \ge 0\} is non‑empty for all \mathbf{x} \in \mathcal{C}. Since \mathcal{U} is convex and the constraint is affine in \mathbf{u} (due to the linear appearance of \mathbf{u} in \dot{\psi}_m for mechanical systems), \Omega(\mathbf{x}) is a convex polytope. Minimising a strictly convex quadratic objective over a non‑empty convex set always yields a unique solution. ∎

For AMPS, the required safe input set is found by off‑line sampling of the digital twin; the existence of a Lipschitz continuous safe policy is confirmed via reinforcement learning of a safety critic, whose solution acts as \mathbf{u}_{\text{safe}} to initialise the QP.

