Appendix E — Iterative AI Design Algorithm (from Reference [5])

The AI‑based design algorithm for protective clothing formalises the design problem as:

1. Hazard vector \mathbf{H} = [H_{\text{thermal}}, H_{\text{mechanical}}, H_{\text{chemical}}, H_{\text{radiation}}].
2. Material capability vector \mathbf{M} = [M_{\text{insulation}}, M_{\text{stiffness}}, M_{\text{permeability}}, M_{\text{attenuation}}].
3. Design solution \mathbf{S} = f_{\text{AI}}(\mathbf{H}, \mathbf{M}), where f_{\text{AI}} is a neural network trained on a combination of experimental data and digital twin simulations.

Three feedback loops operate:

· Loop 1: Prototype testing data is fed back to retrain the forward model.
· Loop 2: Human factors assessments refine the weighting of protection vs. comfort.
· Loop 3: Field data from wearables adjusts the threat‑probability priors.

The convergence proof uses the Robbins–Monro stochastic approximation: the design parameters \mathbf{w} are updated via \mathbf{w}_{k+1} = \mathbf{w}_k - \eta_k \nabla_{\mathbf{w}} \mathcal{L}(\mathbf{w}_k, \text{data}_k), where \eta_k satisfies \sum \eta_k = \infty, \sum \eta_k^2 < \infty. Under typical regularity conditions, \mathbf{w}_k \to \mathbf{w}^* almost surely.

This algorithm is embedded in Proposal 4 and Proposal 6 to reduce the number of physical prototypes needed.

