Appendix H — Multi‑Attribute Trade Study Methodology

The system‑level trade study uses a weighted‑sum multi‑attribute utility function:

U(\mathbf{x}) = \sum_{i=1}^{5} w_i \, u_i(x_i),

with attributes:

1. Mass (kg) — lower is better; utility u_1 = (12 - m)/12.
2. Endurance (h) — u_2 = \min(t/8, 1).
3. Protection Factor — composite score from thermal, mechanical, chemical tests; piecewise linear.
4. Cost — relative to baseline passive suit; utility log‑scaled.
5. Comfort (don/doff time, range of motion) — assessed by user panel.

Weights w_i are elicited from firefighters, soldiers, and industrial hygienists using the Analytic Hierarchy Process (AHP). The trade study compares four configurations:

· Baseline passive: conventional fire‑proximity suit.
· Active SMA continuous: SMA morphing without clutches (fails endurance).
· Clutch‑SMA + PCM only: rebound heating risk.
· Full AMPS v3.0: clutch‑SMA + STF‑auxetic + PCM‑TEC + electroactive barrier.

Only the full AMPS configuration exceeds the minimum utility threshold of 0.85 across all stakeholder groups.